Docker Cheat Sheet

1. Docker Basics
- docker version — show Docker client and server versions.
- docker info — display Docker system-wide information.
- docker login — authenticate to a Docker registry.
- docker logout — remove registry authentication.
- docker help — show Docker command help.

2. Images
- docker pull <image> — download an image from a registry.
- docker images — list local Docker images.
- docker image ls — alias for listing local images.
- docker image inspect <image> — show image metadata.
- docker image rm <image> — remove one or more local images.
- docker image prune — remove unused images.
- docker pull <repo>:<tag> — pull a specific image tag.
- docker tag <source> <target> — add a new tag to an image.
- docker push <image> — upload a tagged image to a registry.

3. Containers
- docker run <image> — create and start a container from an image.
- docker run -d <image> — run a container in detached mode.
- docker run --name <name> <image> — name the container.
- docker run -p <host>:<container> <image> — map host port to container port.
- docker run -e KEY=VALUE <image> — set environment variables for a container.
- docker ps — list running containers.
- docker ps -a — list all containers, including stopped.
- docker start <container> — start a stopped container.
- docker stop <container> — stop a running container gracefully.
- docker restart <container> — restart a container.
- docker rm <container> — remove a stopped container.
- docker rm -f <container> — force remove a container.
- docker run --rm <image> — auto-remove container when it exits.
- docker exec -it <container> <command> — run a command inside a running container.
- docker logs <container> — show container logs.
- docker logs -f <container> — follow container logs in real time.
- docker attach <container> — attach local input/output to a running container.
- docker inspect <container> — show container configuration and state.

4. Build and Dockerfile
- docker build -t <name>:<tag> <path> — build an image from a Dockerfile.
- docker build --no-cache -t <name>:<tag> <path> — build without using cache.
- docker build -f <Dockerfile> -t <name>:<tag> <path> — use a specific Dockerfile.
- docker history <image> — show image layer history.
- docker build --build-arg KEY=VALUE -t <name> <path> — pass build arguments.

5. Volumes & Storage
- docker volume ls — list Docker volumes.
- docker volume create <name> — create a named volume.
- docker volume inspect <name> — inspect volume details.
- docker volume rm <name> — remove a volume.
- docker run -v <volume>:<container_path> <image> — mount a named volume into a container.
- docker run -v <host_path>:<container_path> <image> — mount a host directory into a container.
- docker run --mount type=volume,source=<name>,target=<path> <image> — use the newer mount syntax.

6. Networks
- docker network ls — list Docker networks.
- docker network create <name> — create a custom network.
- docker network inspect <name> — inspect network details.
- docker network rm <name> — remove a Docker network.
- docker run --network <name> <image> — connect a container to a network.
- docker network connect <network> <container> — attach a running container to a network.
- docker network disconnect <network> <container> — detach a container from a network.

7. Compose (Docker Compose)
- docker compose up — build and start services from docker-compose.yml.
- docker compose up -d — start services in detached mode.
- docker compose down — stop and remove containers, networks, and volumes created by Compose.
- docker compose ps — list Compose-managed containers.
- docker compose logs — show logs for compose services.
- docker compose logs -f — follow logs for compose services.
- docker compose build — build or rebuild services.
- docker compose stop — stop services without removing containers.
- docker compose rm — remove stopped service containers.
- docker compose exec <service> <command> — run a command in a running service container.

8. Cleanup and Maintenance
- docker system df — show disk usage of Docker objects.
- docker system prune — remove unused containers, networks, images, and build cache.
- docker system prune -a — remove all unused images as well.
- docker container prune — remove all stopped containers.
- docker image prune -a — remove all dangling and unused images.
- docker network prune — remove unused networks.
- docker volume prune — remove unused volumes.

9. Registry and Authentication
- docker login <registry> — log in to a private registry.
- docker logout <registry> — log out from a registry.
- docker search <term> — search Docker Hub for images.
- docker pull <repository>:<tag> — download an image from the registry.
- docker push <repository>:<tag> — upload an image to the registry.

10. Troubleshooting
- docker stats — display runtime container resource usage.
- docker events — stream Docker events from the daemon.
- docker diff <container> — inspect file system changes made by a container.
- docker commit <container> <new_image> — create a new image from a container’s changes.
- docker cp <container>:<path> <host_path> — copy files from a container to host.
- docker cp <host_path> <container>:<path> — copy files from host into a container.
- docker info | more — paginate Docker info output for easier reading.

11. Common Shortcuts
- docker run -d --name <name> -p 80:80 <image> — start a web container detached with port mapping.
- docker build -t myapp:latest . — build an image named myapp from the current directory.
- docker run --rm -it <image> bash — run an interactive temporary container and remove it after exit.
- docker compose up --build — rebuild services before starting.
- docker image prune --filter "until=24h" — remove images unused for more than 24 hours.

Notes:
- Use `docker ps -a` to see containers that are stopped or exited.
- Use `docker inspect` for deep object details when troubleshooting.
- Use `docker compose` with newer Docker releases instead of `docker-compose` when available.
